    # ── Fever ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Fever (temperature above 38°C/100.4°F) is commonly caused by viral or bacterial infections. The body raises its temperature to fight pathogens. Most fevers in adults resolve within 3-4 days with rest, fluids, and paracetamol. Seek care if fever exceeds 40°C, lasts more than 5 days, or is accompanied by a stiff neck, rash, or difficulty breathing.", "source": "WHO Health Topics", "topic": "fever", "url": "https://www.who.int", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Dengue fever presents with sudden high fever (39-40°C), severe headache, pain behind the eyes, muscle and joint pain, nausea, vomiting, and skin rash. It is transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes. There is no specific antiviral treatment. Management includes rest, oral rehydration, and paracetamol (avoid aspirin/ibuprofen). Warning signs: severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, bleeding gums — go to hospital immediately.", "source": "WHO Dengue Factsheet", "topic": "dengue", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/dengue-and-severe-dengue", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Malaria symptoms include fever with chills, sweating, headache, muscle aches, fatigue, and sometimes nausea or vomiting. Symptoms appear 10-15 days after a mosquito bite. Malaria is a medical emergency — seek diagnosis and treatment immediately. Do not self-treat without confirmation. Prevention: use mosquito nets, repellents, and antimalarial prophylaxis when traveling to endemic regions.", "source": "WHO Malaria Factsheet", "topic": "malaria", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/malaria", "language": "en"},

    # ── Respiratory ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Common cold symptoms include runny or stuffy nose, sneezing, sore throat, mild cough, and low-grade fever. It is caused by rhinoviruses and usually resolves in 7-10 days. Treatment is symptomatic: rest, fluids, decongestants. Antibiotics are not effective against viral infections. See a doctor if symptoms worsen after day 5 or are severe.", "source": "CDC Common Cold", "topic": "cold", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/antibiotic-use/colds.html", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Influenza (flu) causes sudden fever, chills, muscle aches, headache, fatigue, dry cough, and sore throat. Unlike colds, flu onset is abrupt and systemic. Annual vaccination is the most effective prevention. High-risk groups (elderly, pregnant, immunocompromised) should seek antiviral therapy within 48 hours of symptom onset. Seek emergency care for difficulty breathing, persistent chest pain, or confusion.", "source": "CDC Influenza", "topic": "flu", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/flu/symptoms/symptoms.htm", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Asthma is a chronic respiratory condition characterized by episodes of wheezing, shortness of breath, chest tightness, and coughing — often worse at night or with exercise. Triggers include allergens, smoke, cold air, and respiratory infections. Management involves inhaled corticosteroids (controller) and short-acting bronchodilators (rescue). Severe attacks require emergency treatment. Avoid known triggers and follow an asthma action plan.", "source": "WHO Asthma Factsheet", "topic": "asthma", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/asthma", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Pneumonia is an infection that inflames the air sacs in one or both lungs. Symptoms include cough with phlegm, fever, chills, and difficulty breathing. It can be caused by bacteria, viruses, or fungi. Bacterial pneumonia requires antibiotics. High-risk individuals: elderly, infants, immunocompromised. Warning signs: rapid breathing, blue lips, confusion — seek emergency care immediately.", "source": "WHO Pneumonia Factsheet", "topic": "pneumonia", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/pneumonia", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Tuberculosis (TB) is an airborne bacterial infection primarily affecting the lungs. Symptoms: persistent cough (3+ weeks), coughing blood, chest pain, weight loss, night sweats, fatigue, fever. TB is curable with a 6-month antibiotic regimen. Drug-resistant TB requires longer treatment. If you have persistent cough lasting more than 2-3 weeks, get tested. TB spreads through air — proper ventilation and masks reduce transmission.", "source": "WHO TB Factsheet", "topic": "tuberculosis", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/tuberculosis", "language": "en"},

    # ── Chest Pain ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Chest pain can signal heart attack — a medical emergency. Warning signs: pressure, squeezing, fullness, or pain in center or left side of chest lasting more than a few minutes, or spreading to arm, back, neck, jaw, or stomach, plus shortness of breath, cold sweat, nausea, or lightheadedness. Call emergency services immediately. Do not drive yourself to hospital. Chew aspirin 325mg if not allergic.", "source": "CDC Heart Attack", "topic": "chest_pain_cardiac", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/heartdisease/heart_attack.htm", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Not all chest pain is cardiac. Common non-cardiac causes: acid reflux (burning sensation, worse after meals), costochondritis (sharp pain that worsens with movement or touch), anxiety/panic attacks (tight chest with rapid heart rate, shortness of breath), pleuritis (sharp pain that worsens with breathing). However, always rule out cardiac cause first — if in doubt, seek emergency evaluation.", "source": "Mayo Clinic Chest Pain", "topic": "chest_pain_noncardiac", "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/symptoms/chest-pain", "language": "en"},

    # ── Headache ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Tension headache is the most common type: dull, aching pain that feels like a band tightening around the head, often with scalp or neck tenderness. Triggers: stress, poor posture, eye strain, dehydration. Treatment: paracetamol/ibuprofen, hydration, rest, stress management. See a doctor if headaches are frequent (>15 days/month), severe, or not responding to OTC medication.", "source": "WHO Headache Disorders", "topic": "tension_headache", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/headache-disorders", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Migraine is a severe, often one-sided throbbing headache lasting 4-72 hours, often with nausea, vomiting, and sensitivity to light and sound. About 1 in 3 people experience aura (visual disturbances, tingling) before the headache. Triggers: hormonal changes, certain foods (wine, cheese), stress, sleep disruption. Triptans are effective for acute attacks. Preventive medications for frequent migraines.", "source": "WHO Headache Disorders", "topic": "migraine", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/headache-disorders", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Emergency headache warning signs (call emergency services): sudden thunderclap headache (worst of your life), headache with fever and stiff neck (possible meningitis), headache with confusion or altered consciousness, headache after head injury, headache with vision loss or double vision, new severe headache in someone over 50. These require immediate medical evaluation.", "source": "NHS Headache", "topic": "headache_emergency", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/headaches/", "language": "en"},

    # ── Gastrointestinal ──────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Food poisoning symptoms include nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, stomach cramps, and sometimes fever, appearing within hours to days after eating contaminated food. Most cases resolve within 1-3 days with rest and oral rehydration (ORS). Seek care if: symptoms last more than 3 days, severe abdominal pain, blood in stool or vomit, signs of dehydration (dry mouth, no urination, dizziness), or symptoms in vulnerable groups (elderly, infants, pregnant).", "source": "CDC Food Safety", "topic": "food_poisoning", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/foodsafety/symptoms.html", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Acid reflux (GERD) causes heartburn — burning sensation in chest or throat, especially after meals or when lying down. Other symptoms: regurgitation, difficulty swallowing, chronic cough. Lifestyle measures: eat smaller meals, avoid trigger foods (spicy, fatty, citrus, caffeine, alcohol), don't lie down within 2-3 hours of eating, elevate head of bed. Antacids and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) are effective. Persistent symptoms need evaluation to rule out complications.", "source": "Mayo Clinic GERD", "topic": "acid_reflux", "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/gerd", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Diarrhoea is defined as 3 or more loose or watery stools per day. Most acute cases are caused by viral or bacterial infections and resolve within 2-3 days. Key treatment: oral rehydration therapy (ORS) — mix 1 litre clean water + 6 teaspoons sugar + 1/2 teaspoon salt. BRAT diet (banana, rice, applesauce, toast) may help. Seek care if: diarrhoea contains blood or mucus, severe abdominal pain, signs of dehydration, or lasts more than 3 days.", "source": "WHO Diarrhoea", "topic": "diarrhoea", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/diarrhoeal-disease", "language": "en"},

    # ── Diabetes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
