    {"text": "Diabetic emergencies: Hypoglycaemia (low blood sugar <70 mg/dL) — symptoms: shaking, sweating, confusion, hunger, pale skin. Treatment: 15g fast-acting sugar (4 glucose tablets, 150ml juice), recheck in 15 min. Hyperglycaemia (high blood sugar) — symptoms: excessive thirst, frequent urination, fatigue, fruity breath. Diabetic ketoacidosis is life-threatening — seek emergency care if vomiting, abdominal pain, rapid breathing, or confusion.", "source": "CDC Diabetes", "topic": "diabetes_emergency", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/basics/low-blood-sugar.html", "language": "en"},

    # ── Hypertension ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Hypertension (high blood pressure) is a blood pressure consistently ≥140/90 mmHg. It often has no symptoms ('silent killer') but increases risk of heart attack, stroke, and kidney disease. Prevention and management: reduce salt intake (<5g/day), exercise regularly, limit alcohol, quit smoking, maintain healthy weight, manage stress. Medications (ACE inhibitors, ARBs, calcium channel blockers) are often needed. Get BP checked at least once a year.", "source": "WHO Hypertension Factsheet", "topic": "hypertension", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hypertension", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Hypertensive crisis: blood pressure >180/120 mmHg is a medical emergency. Symptoms may include severe headache, shortness of breath, nosebleeds, or no symptoms at all. If accompanied by chest pain, vision changes, or neurological symptoms — call emergency services immediately (hypertensive emergency). Without symptoms — rest 5 minutes and recheck; see doctor same day (hypertensive urgency). Do not abruptly stop blood pressure medications.", "source": "American Heart Association", "topic": "hypertension_crisis", "url": "https://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/high-blood-pressure", "language": "en"},

    # ── Skin ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Chickenpox (varicella) presents as itchy, blister-like rash starting on the face and trunk, spreading over 3-5 days with fever and malaise. Highly contagious until all blisters have crusted. Complications are more severe in adults and immunocompromised individuals. Treatment: antihistamines for itch, paracetamol for fever (avoid aspirin). Acyclovir antiviral for high-risk groups. Vaccine available and highly effective.", "source": "CDC Chickenpox", "topic": "chickenpox", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/chickenpox/about/symptoms.html", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Urticaria (hives) are raised, itchy welts on the skin caused by allergic reactions, infections, stress, or medications. Acute urticaria resolves within 6 weeks; chronic urticaria lasts longer. Treatment: antihistamines (cetirizine, loratadine). Angioedema (swelling of face, lips, throat) with hives is potentially life-threatening — seek emergency care if breathing difficulty or throat swelling.", "source": "NHS Urticaria", "topic": "urticaria", "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hives/", "language": "en"},

    # ── Mental Health ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"text": "Anxiety disorders are among the most common mental health conditions. Symptoms: excessive worry, restlessness, fatigue, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, sleep problems. Treatment options: cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), relaxation techniques, regular exercise, and in some cases medication. Avoid self-medicating with alcohol. If anxiety significantly impairs daily functioning, seek professional help.", "source": "WHO Mental Health", "topic": "anxiety", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-disorders", "language": "en"},
    {"text": "Depression symptoms: persistent sadness, loss of interest in activities, fatigue, changes in appetite or sleep, difficulty concentrating, feelings of worthlessness, and in severe cases, thoughts of self-harm or suicide. Depression is a medical condition, not a personal weakness. Treatment: psychotherapy, antidepressants, lifestyle changes (exercise, social connection). If you have thoughts of self-harm, call a crisis helpline immediately.", "source": "WHO Depression Factsheet", "topic": "depression", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/depression", "language": "en"},
